  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Thallam Puma Chandra Rao vs The State Of Andhra Pradesh on 19 September, 2019 Author: U. Durga Prasad Rao Bench: U. Durga Prasad Rao Hon'Ble Sri Justice U. Durga Prasad Rao Criminal Petition No.5639 Of 2019 Order:",
    "facts": "The petitioners/accused seek to quash the proceedings against them in Crime No.272 of 2019 of Narasaraopet II Town Police Station, Guntur District, registered for the offences under Sections 188, 270, 273, 328 and 420 IPC and Sections 56, 57(1) and 63 of Food Safety and Standards Act (for short, 'FSS Act'). 2. The allegations against the petitioners briefly are that on 12.09.2019 at 06.30 p.m., when the complainant along with his staff proceeded to Industrial Area, Near Varun Water Plant, Narasaraopet, on suspicion, caught the accused and found the banned tobacco products in their possession and hence, arrested them and seized the same and registered a case in Crime No.272 of 2019 for the offences stated supra. 3. Learned counsel for petitioner while denying the FIR allegations would submit that even if the prosecution case is accepted to be true, tobacco products would not fall within the definition of Section 3(j) of FSS Act and therefore, the Police are not authorized to register the crime, investigate and file charge sheet and therefore, registration of the FIR is contrary to the provisions of FSS Act. He relied upon the order of this Court in Crl.P.Nos.3731 of 2018 and batch, dated 27.08.2018.",
    "arguments": "4. So far as the offence under Section 188 of IPC is concerned, as per Section 195(1)(a)(i) of Cr.P.C, no Court shall take cognizance of the said offence except on the complaint in writing of that Court or by such Officer of the Court as that Court may authorise in writing in this behalf, or of some other Court to which that Court is subordinate.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "4. So far as the offence under Section 188 of IPC is concerned, as per Section 195(1)(a)(i) of Cr.P.C, no Court shall take cognizance of the said offence except on the complaint in writing of that Court or by such Officer of the Court as that Court may authorise in writing in this behalf, or of some other Court to which that Court is subordinate."
  },
